 5	## Conventions
 6	
 7	Definitions stay beside their implementation unless independent consumers need a neutral contract. Feature contracts belong to the feature and external callers use explicit public entries. Portable UI controls own their structural prop and style types: primitives belong to `client/ui/elements`, package-wide host contracts belong to `client/ui/hosts`, portable platform state belongs to `client/ui/environment`, domain-neutral composition contracts belong to `client/ui/compositions`, generic DOM-action contracts belong to `client/ui/actions`, and visual asset contracts belong with `client/ui/icons`, `client/ui/illustrations`, or `client/ui/branding`. Those contracts must not derive from schema records, DataFn resources, runtime types, application actions, or state interfaces; owning adapters translate domain models at the UI boundary. Keyboard contracts are shared by controls and application shortcuts. Application action contracts belong to client configuration; shared command-host and event contracts belong to `client/shared-state/commands` and `client/shared-state/events`; resource-panel and notification models belong to their named capabilities. Browser ambient declarations live in client/ambient.
